{"author":{"id":"127a58c7f014820991be95805df42525db12c92e2d7319d04d4df105a9aa3444"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1788369232,"nonce":"QUOcPpqgOLafmQpxk0H+YuD5CW8=","title":"Found while measuring `f08dfc4` against `transcripts/m1-20260901*`, and it is the cause of a 60-turn wedge nobody has attributed yet.","message":"```\nm1-20260901-s02/695-advance  -\u003e  m1-20260901-s03/047-advance\n60 consecutive `advance {until:{letter:true}, timeout_ticks:60000}`\n60 consecutive refusals, error.code \"unread-journal\", byte-identical detail\nTicksGame 3,704,384 at the first.  TicksGame 3,704,384 at the last.\nfive minutes of wall clock, sixty turns, ZERO in-game ticks\n```\n\nThe loop was not ignoring the guard. It called `journal` between every single pair. It called it like this, every turn:\n\n```json\n{\"op\":\"journal\",\"args\":{\"since_seq\":0,\"limit\":2000}}\n-\u003e {\"ok\":true,\"data\":{\"count\":2000,\"read_watermark\":2024,\"unread_after\":9}}\n```\n\nFrom seq 0 the `limit` is exhausted at seq 2024, nine rows short of the tail. A truncated read correctly moves the watermark only as far as the rows it handed over, so the four events blocking the advance — seq 2025..2028, **`death 1, letter 3`** — were never read. A colonist death the run never saw, which is `722c951`'s founding case exactly.\n\n**The mod was right sixty times.** `unread_after: 9` was in every reply. So this is not a correctness bug; it is `eef837a`'s shape — *a truthful field that does not say the thing the caller needs* — and `d9d6c12`'s and `253c694`'s. The reply says `ok: true` and `count: 2000`, both of which read as \"I read the journal\", and the one field that says otherwise is a number in the middle of six.\n\nThe blocking read has three ways to come back short and the envelope distinguishes none of them: `limit` exhausted, `types` filtered, `since_tick` scoped. `rwa/README.md` already documents that all three under-move the watermark; nothing in the ENVELOPE says which one just happened, or that one did.\n\n`f08dfc4`'s `repeated` counter (shipped) catches the SYMPTOM on turn three. It cannot name the cause — diagnosing this one took reading five transcript segments.\n\n\n1. A `journal` reply whose read did not reach the tail carries a field the caller cannot read as clean — working name `truncated`, absent on a complete read — naming **which** limit bit (`limit` / `types` / `since_tick`) and how many rows are still unread. `unread_after` stays as it is; this is the statement that the number is a consequence of the CALL and not of the world.\n2. It names the exact next call: `journal {since_seq:\u003cread_watermark\u003e}`. The run's mistake was paging from 0 every turn, and the fix is one field the caller can copy.\n3. **A tri-state, not a bool**, per this round's B-1 rule and `f9dadc7`: \"complete\", \"short and here is why\", and \"not applicable\" (a read that asked for nothing) must not collapse. An ABSENT key must not be the way \"short\" is spelled — `eef837a` cost twenty days to exactly that.\n4. `advance`'s `unread-journal` detail already tells the caller to run `journal {since_seq:\u003cwatermark\u003e}`. Cross-check that the two agree on the number, since a caller that follows one and not the other is the loop this issue is about.\n5. Bench command list, `accept/\u003cid\u003e-*.md` (no Python suite this round): journal a handful of events, read with `limit` one short of the tail, assert the field appears and names `limit`; read with `types` excluding a journaled type, assert it names `types`; read to the tail, assert it is absent and the next `advance` is not refused.\n\n\n`checklists/turn.md` `repeated-refusal` now carries the diagnosis in prose, which is the right place for the human gate and the wrong place for an unattended agent — a derived fact that exists only if the caller remembers to derive it will eventually not be derived (`f9dadc7`'s argument, and this run is the second time it has been paid for).","files":null}]}